} "Athletics"] "\n" [:option {:value "BBL"} "Baseball"] "\n" [:option {:value "PEL"} "Basque pelota"] "\n" [:option {:value "CKT"} "Cricket"] "\n" [:option {:value "CQT"} "Croquet"] "\n" [:option {:value "CTR"} "Cycling Track"] "\n" [:option {:value "EDR"} "Equestrian Dressage"] "\n" [:option {:value "EDV"} "Equestrian Driving"] "\n" [:option {:value "EJP"} "Equestrian Jumping"] "\n" [:option {:value "FEN"} "Fencing"] "\n" [:option {:value "FBL"} "Football"] "\n" [:option {:value "GLF"} "Golf"] "\n" [:option {:value "PBT"} "Motorboating"] "\n" [:option {:value "POL"} "Polo"] "\n" [:option {:value "ROW"} "Rowing"] "\n" [:option {:value "RGB"} "Rugby"] "\n" [:option {:value "SAL"} "Sailing"] "\n" [:option {:value "SWM"} "Swimming"] "\n" [:option {:value "TEN"} "Tennis"] "\n" [:option {:value "TOW"} "Tug-Of-War"] "\n" [:option {:value "WPO"} "Water Polo"]]] [:script {} "$('#sports_in_same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/editions/2/sports/'+$('#sports_in_same_games_select').val()})"] [:form {:id "other_games_form", :class "form-inline", :style "display: inline", :method "get"} [:select {:name "year", :id "sport_in_other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "Shooting in other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "76"} "1875 Zappas Olympic Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "77"} "1888-89 Zappas Olympic Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "1"} "1896 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "4"} "1906  Intercalated Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "5"} "1908 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "6"} "1912 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "7"} "1920 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "8"} "1924 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "10"} "1932 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "11"} "1936 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "12"} "1948 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "13"} "1952 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "14"} "1956 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "15"} "1960 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "16"} "1964 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "17"} "1968 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "18"} "1972 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "19"} "1976 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "20"} "1980 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "21"} "1984 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "22"} "1988 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "23"} "1992 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "24"} "1996 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "25"} "2000 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "26"} "2004 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "53"} "2008 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "65"} "2010 Summer Youth Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "54"} "2012 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "67"} "2014 Summer Youth Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "59"} "2016 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "69"} "2018 Summer Youth Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "61"} "2020 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "63"} "2024 Summer Olympics"]]] [:script {} "$('#sport_in_other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/editions/' + $('#sport_in_other_games_select').val() + '/sports/SHO'})"] "\n" [:h1 {} "Shooting at the 1900 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:table {:class "biodata"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Dates"] "\n" [:td {} "25 June –  7 August 1900"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Medal Events"] "\n" [:td {} "8"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Non-medal Events"] "\n" [:td {} "45"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Venues"] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/205"} "Camp de Satory, Versailles"] " / " [:a {:href "/venues/207"} "Le Stand de l'Île Séguin, Billancourt"]] "\n"] "\n"]] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "The 1900 shooting events are incredibly confusing. Multiple shooting contests were held during the Paris Exposition in 1900. The " [:strong {} "1900 Official Report"] " discusses them in varying degrees of detail, but not all of them can be truly termed Olympic events. Many of them were limited to French shooting societies or were handicap events. Some were open only to certain classes of shooters."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In fact, it could be argued that none of the 1900 shooting events should be considered Olympic events because virtually all of them awarded some form of prize money and were, at least titularly, contested by professionals. " [:em {} "Shooting and Fishing"] " noted, “The big amount of money to be divided as prizes decided many marksmen to enter in the different competitions”. Apparently, this has been known for many years, and the " [:a {:href "/organizations/1"} "IOC"] " has continued to accept several of the events as being part of the 1900 Olympics."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Following, we have listed the 38 shooting events which are discussed in the 1900 Official Report. Of these, we would consider only eight of them to be of “Olympic caliber”."] "\n\n" [:p {} "As noted above, many of these events awarded prize money, which was antithetical to Olympic principles in 1900, except for fencing, in which professionals were allowed to compete. A purist could demur, and state that none of the events awarding prize money should be considered as an “Olympic event”. If that were the case, then only six events would remain – " [:a {:href "/results/51510"} "International Individual Free Rifle Match, Three Positions"] "; " [:a {:href "/results/51511"} "International Individual Free Rifle Match, Standing"] "; " [:a {:href "/results/51512"} "International Individual Free Rifle Match, Kneeling"] "; " [:a {:href "/results/51513"} "International Individual Free Rifle Match, Prone"] "; " [:a {:href "/results/51509"} "International Individual Free Pistol Match"] "; and the " [:a {:href "/results/51514"} "International Trap Shooting"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Further, in the International Free Rifle and International Free Pistol events, the individual shooters received no prize money for their individual performances, but did receive money for the team event, effectively making them professional. Omitting those events would then leave only the International Trap Shooting as a truly amateur event, which would meet Olympic principles as of 1900. It seems more reasonable, if one accepts the above six events as Olympic events, to also include the " [:a {:href "/results/51516"} "International Team Free Rifle"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/51515"} "International Team Free Pistol"] " matches, on which the individual events were based. This would give eight Olympic events for 1900 shooting."] "\n\n" [:p {} "To some extent, it may depend on whether one is a “lumper or a splitter”. We have kept eight shooting events as “Olympic events”, as described above, although we could hardly argue with historians who would contend with that approach. The decision cannot be made categorically. However, as the fourth World Championships were incorporated into the Paris Games, the one and only time the Worlds have run concurrently with the Olympics (and four of the eight Olympic events were included in the World Championship programme), it adds further justification for them being included in the Olympics."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Three defending individual World Champions from 1899 won gold medals in Paris: " [:a {:href "/athletes/41792"} "Lars Jørgen Madsen"] " of Denmark, and the Swiss pair of " [:a {:href "/athletes/44109"} "Konrad Stäheli"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/44052"} "Franz Böckli"] ", although Stäheli was the only one to win the same event in both years, the 300 metres Free Rifle, Kneeling."] "\n\n" [:p {} "More recent Olympic statistical summaries, notably those of " [:a {:href "/athletes/1200376"} "Kamper"] ", Kluge, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/1200825"} "Wallechinsky"] ", have been reasonably accurate in terms of covering the “well-known” 1900 shooting events (Kluge and Wallechinsky certainly used Kamper’s " [:strong {} "Encyclopädia"] " of the Olympic Games as a source.)  However, some of the confusion concerning 1900 shooting must be traced to the early Olympic historians who completely botched the reporting of this sport. Fritz Wasner, in " [:strong {} "Olympia-Lexikon"] ", listed only 10 shooting events as having been held in 1900, omitting many of the 38 events which were conducted. But that was not so bad, as Kamper listed 11, and that has been the standard listing for many years."] "\n\n" [:p {} "It was left to the Hungarian Ferenc Mező, whose book " [:strong {} "The Modern Olympic Games"] " is normally reasonably accurate, to thoroughly confuse many future Olympic and shooting historians. Mező listed 26 shooting events. Of these, 11 were contested as part of the 1900 shooting competitions. The other 15 were contested at the " [:a {:href "/editions/5/sports/SHO"} "1908 Olympics"] " and Mező had them at the wrong Olympic Games. In several cases, however, he did note, “[shooter’s name] won the same event at the 1908 Olympic Games”. What he failed to notice was that, in all 15 cases, the same shooter or nation not only won the same event at the 1908 Olympics, but did so with the exact same score. Mező listed all the same events and winners in his section on 1908."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Still, confusion over the 1900 shooting events is almost excusable. Although archery was similar in that thousands of French archers participated, the 1900 shooting events had a huge turn-out of shooters, mostly from France. It should be noted that, of the 6,351 shooters listed, a full 96% were from France. In fact, the 1900 Official Report noted that 6,351 shooters competed, and 518,602 shots were fired during the competitions."] "\n\n" [:p {} "We did not locate all of these shooters. However, following are the most complete results ever seen for 1900 shooting. In a few of the events, we were able to locate all competitors, thanks to a French shooting magazine from 1900 entitled " [:em {} "Le Stand"] ". Another, presumably more complete, 1900 French shooting magazine existed, entitled " [:em {} "Le Tir National"] ". However, the holdings for this magazine at the Bibliothéque Nationale do not begin until 1901, and we were unable to locate another source for this magazine. The complete results of the 1900 shooting contests were apparently recorded, however. The " [:strong {} "1900 Official Report"] " notes, on page 210, as follows, “Les résultats techniques du concours sont consignés dans un palmarès formant un volume de 300 pages et contenant l’attribution détaillée des 11,000 prix décernés”. Unfortunately, the Bibliothéque Nationale knows nothing of this book, and no other source for it could be found."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The multiple events conducted during the 1900 Paris Exposition Universelle, as described in the 1900 Official Report, and the 1900 Rules and Programmes, were as follows:"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Prix d’Excellence"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Concours d’Excellence"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Roi de Tir (King of Shooting), Rifle France"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Roi de Tir (King of Shooting), Revolver, France"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Military Rifle, 200 metres  [Category 1]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Military Rifle, 200 metres  [Category 2]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Free Rifle, 200 metres  [Category 3]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Free Rifle, 200 metres  [Category 4]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Free Rifle, 300 metres  [Category 5]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Military Pistol, 20 metres  [Category 6]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Free Pistol, 50 metres  [Category 7]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Small-bore Rifle, 12 metres  [Category 8]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Small-bore Rifle, 12 metres  [Category 9]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Running Wild Boar Shooting, 27 metres  [Category 10]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Military Pistol  [Category 11A]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Military Pistol, Silhouette  [Category 11B]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Military Rifle, Youth  [Category 12]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Military Rifle, Adult, Society Championships  [Category 13]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Society Contests, Youth  [Category 14]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Union Contests  [Category 15]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Military Rifle, National  [Category 16]"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Military Pistol, National  [Category 17]"] "\